1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,838
A drawer slides out.

2
00:00:01,838 --> 00:00:02,798
A fan spins.

3
00:00:02,798 --> 00:00:04,671
A wheel rolls down the road.

4
00:00:05,071 --> 00:00:06,043
Three motions.

5
00:00:06,043 --> 00:00:09,267
But physics needs just two
ideas to describe them all.

6
00:00:09,717 --> 00:00:11,430
Aarav tries it in his room.

7
00:00:11,430 --> 00:00:15,117
He pushes a book, swings his
door, and rolls his cycle wheel.

8
00:00:15,467 --> 00:00:19,986
On the door, the handle edge travels
farther than a point near the hinge.

9
00:00:19,986 --> 00:00:22,096
Yet both start and stop together.

10
00:00:22,096 --> 00:00:22,996
How?

11
00:00:23,072 --> 00:00:25,267
To see why, meet the rigid body.

12
00:00:25,867 --> 00:00:27,911
A rigid body keeps its shape.

13
00:00:27,911 --> 00:00:31,582
The distance between any two
of its points never changes.

14
00:00:31,932 --> 00:00:36,185
Real bodies bend a tiny bit, but
a book or a door is close enough.

15
00:00:36,685 --> 00:00:39,531
A rigid body moves in two basic ways.

16
00:00:39,531 --> 00:00:41,549
Translation, and rotation.

17
00:00:41,899 --> 00:00:44,540
In translation, the whole body shifts.

18
00:00:44,540 --> 00:00:48,234
In rotation, it turns about
a line, called the axis.

19
00:00:48,834 --> 00:00:51,970
Let's mark three dots on
Aarav's book: A, B and C.

20
00:00:52,320 --> 00:00:55,021
He pushes it at a steady
thirty centimetres per second.

21
00:00:55,421 --> 00:00:59,416
All three dots have the same
velocity, at every moment.

22
00:00:59,816 --> 00:01:02,950
On the graph, their three
lines sit on top of each other.

23
00:01:03,400 --> 00:01:05,197
This is pure translation.

24
00:01:05,197 --> 00:01:10,083
Every point moves the same way, and
every line stays parallel to itself.

25
00:01:10,683 --> 00:01:12,605
Now the door, from above.

26
00:01:12,605 --> 00:01:15,630
The hinge is the axis, and it stays fixed.

27
00:01:15,980 --> 00:01:18,596
Dot A is thirty
centimetres from the hinge.

28
00:01:18,596 --> 00:01:19,496
B is sixty.

29
00:01:19,486 --> 00:01:21,775
C, at the handle edge, is ninety.

30
00:01:22,175 --> 00:01:24,804
The door swings through
ninety degrees in two seconds.

31
00:01:25,204 --> 00:01:29,088
Every dot moves on a circle,
with its centre on the axis.

32
00:01:29,488 --> 00:01:30,988
Now the graph splits.

33
00:01:30,988 --> 00:01:33,600
C covers three times the distance of A.

34
00:01:34,100 --> 00:01:35,151
Slow-mo replay.

35
00:01:35,151 --> 00:01:36,051
Freeze.

36
00:01:36,150 --> 00:01:39,727
All three dots have turned
through the same angle, theta.

37
00:01:40,127 --> 00:01:44,729
But C is three times farther out,
so its arc, and its arrow, are three

38
00:01:44,729 --> 00:01:45,717
times longer.

39
00:01:46,317 --> 00:01:47,224
Quick check.

40
00:01:47,224 --> 00:01:49,150
Swing the door twice as fast.

41
00:01:49,150 --> 00:01:52,235
Will C still cover three
times the distance of A?

42
00:01:52,235 --> 00:01:53,432
Pause and decide.

43
00:01:56,532 --> 00:01:57,432
Yes!

44
00:01:57,030 --> 00:02:01,597
The dots always turn together, and
C is always three times farther out.

45
00:02:02,197 --> 00:02:04,757
Let's write this down, one step at a time.

46
00:02:05,107 --> 00:02:06,007
Step one.

47
00:02:05,920 --> 00:02:10,502
In translation, every point has the
same displacement in the same time.

48
00:02:10,502 --> 00:02:12,178
So, the same velocity.

49
00:02:12,928 --> 00:02:13,828
Step two.

50
00:02:13,757 --> 00:02:18,342
In rotation about a fixed axis, every
point turns through the same angle

51
00:02:18,342 --> 00:02:20,377
theta, in the same time t.

52
00:02:21,127 --> 00:02:22,027
Step three.

53
00:02:22,022 --> 00:02:25,642
A point at distance r from
the axis moves along an arc.

54
00:02:25,642 --> 00:02:28,396
s equals r theta, with theta in radians.

55
00:02:29,146 --> 00:02:30,083
Step four.

56
00:02:30,083 --> 00:02:33,379
Its speed is s over t, or r theta over t.

57
00:02:33,379 --> 00:02:35,866
Double r, and the speed doubles.

58
00:02:36,616 --> 00:02:39,494
A point on the axis has r equal to zero.

59
00:02:39,494 --> 00:02:40,847
It does not move.

60
00:02:41,447 --> 00:02:42,528
Remember this.

61
00:02:42,528 --> 00:02:45,741
In translation, every
point moves the same way.

62
00:02:45,741 --> 00:02:49,369
In rotation, every point
turns through the same angle.

63
00:02:50,069 --> 00:02:52,232
Now the rolling wheel, link by link.

64
00:02:52,582 --> 00:02:55,313
The axle moves forward in a straight line.

65
00:02:55,313 --> 00:02:56,993
That part is translation.

66
00:02:57,393 --> 00:03:00,206
And every rim point goes round the axle.

67
00:03:00,206 --> 00:03:01,844
That part is rotation.

68
00:03:02,244 --> 00:03:04,324
So rolling is both together.

69
00:03:04,324 --> 00:03:08,174
The axle draws a line, while
a rim dot draws arches.

70
00:03:08,774 --> 00:03:09,827
Side by side.

71
00:03:09,827 --> 00:03:12,986
A sliding book: every
point moves the same way.

72
00:03:13,386 --> 00:03:17,709
A swinging door: every point turns
the same angle, round the hinge.

73
00:03:18,310 --> 00:03:19,338
Now you try.

74
00:03:19,338 --> 00:03:22,724
A giant wheel turns, and
each cabin hangs upright.

75
00:03:22,724 --> 00:03:25,291
Is a cabin in translation or rotation?

76
00:03:25,291 --> 00:03:26,569
Pause and decide.

77
00:03:29,769 --> 00:03:30,669
Translation!

78
00:03:30,627 --> 00:03:34,804
The cabin stays upright, and every
point of it moves the same way.

79
00:03:34,804 --> 00:03:37,416
Translation does not need a straight line.

80
00:03:38,066 --> 00:03:39,756
Now, one worked example.

81
00:03:40,106 --> 00:03:43,806
A door eighty centimetres wide swings
shut through ninety degrees in two

82
00:03:43,806 --> 00:03:44,706
seconds.

83
00:03:44,407 --> 00:03:47,022
How far, and how fast, does its edge move?

84
00:03:47,522 --> 00:03:51,666
Ninety degrees is pi by two, about
one point five seven radians.

85
00:03:52,416 --> 00:03:54,526
Distance: s equals r theta.

86
00:03:54,526 --> 00:03:59,790
Zero point eight times one point five
seven, about one point two six metres.

87
00:04:00,540 --> 00:04:05,138
Speed: one point two six over two,
about zero point six three metres per

88
00:04:05,138 --> 00:04:06,038
second.

89
00:04:06,535 --> 00:04:10,071
A point twenty centimetres from
the hinge is a quarter as far out.

90
00:04:10,071 --> 00:04:11,843
So it moves a quarter as fast.

91
00:04:12,493 --> 00:04:13,954
Now, the exam trap.

92
00:04:14,304 --> 00:04:18,705
Many students say a rolling wheel is
in pure rotation, because it spins.

93
00:04:19,105 --> 00:04:21,367
But its axle moves forward too.

94
00:04:21,367 --> 00:04:23,880
Rolling is translation plus rotation.

95
00:04:24,530 --> 00:04:25,834
Where is this asked?

96
00:04:25,834 --> 00:04:30,008
In concept questions: translation,
rotation or both, and the speed of each

97
00:04:30,008 --> 00:04:30,908
point.

98
00:04:31,142 --> 00:04:32,178
Exam level now.

99
00:04:32,178 --> 00:04:35,258
A door one point two metres
wide turns on its hinge.

100
00:04:35,258 --> 00:04:39,278
In half a second, a point thirty
centimetres from the hinge moves fifteen

101
00:04:39,278 --> 00:04:40,849
centimetres along its arc.

102
00:04:40,849 --> 00:04:43,921
How far does the edge move,
and through what angle?

103
00:04:43,921 --> 00:04:45,000
Pause and solve.

104
00:04:50,400 --> 00:04:51,496
The answer is A.

105
00:04:51,496 --> 00:04:53,671
Sixty centimetres, and half a radian.

106
00:04:54,171 --> 00:04:55,667
Theta is s over r.

107
00:04:55,667 --> 00:04:57,946
Point one five over point three.

108
00:04:57,946 --> 00:05:00,278
Half a radian, for every point.

109
00:05:00,778 --> 00:05:02,831
The edge is four times farther out.

110
00:05:02,831 --> 00:05:05,045
Four times fifteen is sixty centimetres.

111
00:05:05,645 --> 00:05:07,185
Here is your recap.

112
00:05:07,185 --> 00:05:08,768
Screenshot this card.

113
00:05:11,968 --> 00:05:13,906
Can you answer these without help?

114
00:05:14,306 --> 00:05:15,206
One.

115
00:05:14,774 --> 00:05:17,370
Why is a giant wheel cabin in translation?

116
00:05:18,270 --> 00:05:19,170
Two.

117
00:05:18,662 --> 00:05:21,833
Why does a door's edge move faster
than a point near the hinge?

118
00:05:22,733 --> 00:05:23,633
Three.

119
00:05:23,229 --> 00:05:25,660
Why is a rolling wheel
not in pure rotation?

120
00:05:26,560 --> 00:05:29,725
Next time: Diya balances
a ruler on one finger.

121
00:05:29,725 --> 00:05:33,094
Add coins to one end, and
the balance point moves.

122
00:05:33,094 --> 00:05:34,246
Centre of mass.
